"""Manual mathematical witnesses, in the source order of formal tasks per section.
These are audit records, not automatically inferred solutions.
"""
S={}
def add(n,s): S[n]=[x.strip() for x in s.split('||')]
add(1,'''Equal displacement preserves the separation S-R, so destinations differ; (4,4) specifies change, not starting position. || Temperature returns a scalar; wind returns magnitude and direction. || Specify the direction of the step; opposite directions on a line share one derivative sign convention, while a hillside has independent directions.''')
add(2,'''c>0 preserves direction, c<0 reverses it, c=0 gives zero. || Choose both coefficients zero. || Translation changes the base point; sets coincide iff P=t0 u, since P+t u=(t+t0)u.''')
add(3,'''x2 tells the signed multiple of the pure second-coordinate unit step e2. || Parametric form produces points by choosing t; implicit form tests membership by substitution. || Addition and scaling are defined entry by entry and do not require a drawing.''')
add(4,'''Give the zero vector coefficient 1 and every other vector coefficient 0. || Subtract two expansions: independence forces every coefficient difference zero; spanning gives existence. || The exchange argument bounds any independent list by the three standard basis directions; four listed vectors need not be independent.''')
add(5,'''Norm is zero exactly for the zero displacement X-C. || Equality requires the lake on the straight segment from cabin to lookout, including coincident stops; the prompt omits the zero-vector exception. || Division by zero is undefined, and zero has no direction for a unit representative.''')
add(6,'''Choose any P on the same line and any nonzero multiple of u; reparametrize t. || An in-plane step keeps A dx+B dy+C dz=0; a step in n changes the expression by A²+B²+C²>0. || Solve for one coordinate with nonzero coefficient; the other n-1 are free. All-zero coefficients give all space or the empty set.''')
add(7,'''Point is location; vector is displacement. || Q-P=(5,4,-4) records coordinate changes independently of the origin. || Parallel equal arrows encode the same coordinate change. || It has form a u+b v for real a,b. || (4,2)=2(2,1). || e1,e2: a e1+b e2=0 forces a=b=0. || e1,e2,e1+e2 with relation e1+e2-(e1+e2)=0. || Spanning and linear independence. || Zero norm prevents division and there is no direction. || Circle boundary radius3 centered(1,-2), versus its interior together with boundary. || Parametric form generates points; normal equation tests membership and gives a perpendicular direction. || It says 0=4, so has no points. || u+v=(1,4,4), 2u-3v=(12,-17,8), -u=(-3,1,-4). || Norms13,3,2. || sqrt(4²+(-3)²+6²)=sqrt61. || (x-2)²+(y+1)²+(z-4)²=36. || Center(3,0,-5), radius7. || (4/5,0,-3/5). || (1,0,2)+t(3,-1,4). || Yes, all coordinates give t=2. || (0,1,2)+s(1,0,3)+t(2,1,0). || Use(s,t)=(0,0),(1,0),(0,1): (0,1,2),(1,1,5),(2,2,2). || 2(x-1)-(y-2)+5(z-3)=0. || No: left side7, not9. || (4-2s+t,s,t). || abs(2-2+6-7)/sqrt9=1/3. || Squared distance=(2t-3)²+(t-4)²=5(t-2)²+5; nearest(4,2),distance sqrt5. || Elimination gives s=(3x-y)/5,t=(-x+2y)/5; every(x,y) is reached. || Reach exactly y=x/2; coefficient s+2t controls the single direction. || Coordinates(s,t,s+t), hence z=x+y. || (2+s,t,1+s+t), equation z=x+y-1. || A plane has a two-vector spanning basis; exchange bound forbids three independent vectors. || Contrapositive of the preceding dimension argument. || Coordinates(2,2),(3,-3),(3,2), from s=(x+y)/2,t=(x-y)/2. || Diagonal orthogonal grid, rotated45 degrees and scaled by sqrt2. || Horizontal force does no work on vertical displacement; answers0 and30. Candidate scalar rule Fx dx+Fy dy; this is guided discovery, not an assumed dot-product theorem. || Bounding rectangle minus triangles gives area5; candidate signed rule a1 b2-a2 b1. Discovery asks an estimate/conjecture, not a proof of the later determinant theorem. || Area product should negate under interchange. || A nonzero quantity cannot be both symmetric and antisymmetric; alignment and oriented area need different parts.''')
add(8,'''The already computed map(a,b)->(-b,a) preserves squared norm and sends e1 to e2; together with the chosen Euclidean coordinates it is a positive quarter turn. The relation i²=-1 alone would not characterize a Euclidean rotation. || Polar multiplication composes positive scalings and angle additions, as the local angle-addition computation proves; natural here means this geometric interpretation, not uniqueness among every conceivable product. || The multiplication scheme on 1,i,j needs the extra product direction k to close. The historical/geometric prompt is motivational, not a proof of a classification theorem.''')
add(9,'''Right multiply (ij)k=-1 by k and use k²=-1 to obtain ij=k. The prompt names i² but the necessary k² is supplied in the full displayed rule and hint. || Spatial rotations about different axes have order-dependent outcomes, so an order-sensitive product can record them. || Addition and real scaling are componentwise and V²=-norm(v)².''')
add(10,'''Diagonal terms ui vi i² etc contribute negative matching-component products; mixed terms have no scalar part. || Reversing order reverses the oriented parallelogram normal; ij=k versus ji=-k models the sign. || Half-sum=-dot is alignment; half-difference=cross is oriented area, unchanged versus negated by swapping.''')
add(11,'''Nonzero lengths let one divide to obtain cos theta=0, hence theta=pi/2; zero vectors do not define such an angle. || Projection=(v·u/u·u)u lies on the line; dotting the residual with u gives zero. || For nonzero vectors abs(cos theta)<=1, equality at0 orpi; a zero input also gives equality.''')
add(12,'''The computed vector is orthogonal to both edge directions; a normal encodes the oriented plane without choosing an in-plane edge direction. || Magnitude is parallelogram area; direction is its right-hand normal. Swap keeps magnitude and negates vector. || Orthogonal complement of a plane has dimensions0,1,2 in R2,R3,R4; only R3 has the required single normal line. Scope is the plane-area normal construction, not every generalized algebraic cross product.''')
add(13,'''Positive and negative signed areas distinguish counterclockwise and clockwise ordered edge pairs. || Interchanging two columns negates the coordinate determinant; two swaps restore sign. The earlier determinant formula, not continuity-through-zero alone, justifies the sign. || Image edges(2h,h),(k,3k) have determinant5hk; divide by hk to get5.''')
add(14,'''B(u,u)=-B(u,u), so 2B(u,u)=0; the degenerate parallelogram has zero area. || Evaluate both on(u,v): u1v2-u2v1 and its negative, unlike commutative scalar multiplication. || Ordered values5 and-5; ordinary area is5.''')
add(15,'''Images of1,i are(c,s),(-s,c); general image(cx-sy,sx+cy) has squared norm x²+y² and angle alpha+phi. At phi=pi/6 both basis arrows turn30 degrees. || qj images: c+sj,ci-sk,-s+cj,si+ck. qk images: c+sk,ci+sj,-si+cj,-s+ck. Each ordered plane pair has the standard2D rotation matrix; reversing its order negates angle. Sum the two preserved squared plane norms. || Real part=-sx; at phi=pi/2, i maps to-1, outside imaginary space although four-dimensional norm is preserved. || (a0+V)(a0-V)=a0²-V²=norm(q)² in either order; inverse=conjugate/norm² and qi inverse=c-si. || Right images c-si,s+ci,cj+sk,-sj+ck: angles -phi in(1,i),+phi in(j,k), so combined0 and2phi. || Conjugation fixes1,i; j goes cos2phi j+sin2phi k and k goes -sin2phi j+cos2phi k. Linearity gives no scalar part. || phi=theta/2; q=(1+i)/sqrt2 sends i+2j+3k to i-3j+2k, squared norm14. q=i gives pi and j->-j. Complex commutativity cancels the conjugating factors. || Cyclic replacement gives abouty:(x cos theta+z sin theta,y,-x sin theta+z cos theta); aboutz:(x cos theta-y sin theta,x sin theta+y cos theta,z). Positive quarter turns k->i,i->-k and i->j,j->-i respectively; right multiplication cancels axis-plane turn and adds perpendicular-plane turn. || U,B perpendicular unit implies UB=W=u×b, BU=-W, and W unit perpendicular to both. UW=-B,WU=B by the explicitly invoked associativity in prompt/hint, or direct table expansion. Left/right rotation tables in(1,U),(B,W) reproduce0,2phi under conjugation; W=u×b gives right-hand orientation. || u·vperp=0, cross with vparallel=0. Rotate the perpendicular component in orthogonal equal-length pair(vperp,u×vperp); fix parallel component, giving Rodrigues formula and preservation of norm and axial component. Zero perpendicular component is fixed directly. || q=(1+i+j+k)/2 cycles i->j->k->i; V maps to3i+j+2k, squarednorm14 and axis component6/sqrt3. (-q)^-1=-q^-1 gives same conjugation. theta2pi givesq=-1 and identity. xtheny sendsk->-j, reversed sendsk->i; associative multiplication and reverse-order inverses give qy qx.''')
add(16,'''Position versus displacement; same coordinate tuple has different role. || Scale by r and rotate by theta. || Unit table has ij=-ji; ordered spatial rotations need order-sensitive algebra. || Reversed oriented normal. || Scalar part -dot is alignment; vector cross part is oriented area. || Perpendicular nonzero vectors. || Linear dependence, including zero inputs. || Coordinate cross formula has dot products zero with both edges. || Swap negates determinant while absolute area is unchanged. || Zero scalar triple product means height above the span plane vanishes, hence no3D volume. || Its determinant relative to(i,j,k) is positive. || Two independent normal directions remain for a plane in R4, no unique normal-line construction. || Interchanging the covector order negates signed area evaluation. || dx(u)dx(v)-dx(v)dx(u)=0. || 5+i; lengths multiply to sqrt26 and arguments add. || 2(cos(2pi/3)+i sin(2pi/3)); scale2,rotate120degrees. || -4-14i-j+9k; scalar-4, vector(-14,-1,9). || Dot11,norms5,3,cosine11/15. || (11/9)(1,2,2). || Work300 joules. || (-14,-1,9); dot products -28+1+27=0 and-14-4+18=0. || (6,-3,1). || 6(x-2)-3(y+1)+(z-4)=0, or6x-3y+z=19. || (0,0,10). || 18, positive orientation. || Edges(4,1),(1,5), determinant19; triangle area19/2. || Determinant13. || Absolute determinant13 times supplied tetrahedron factor1/6 gives13/6. || Determinant10. || Positive10 preserves orientation. || 4*3-(-1)*2=14. || Shadows(-12,3,7), exactly the cross components. || Positive,negative,notbasis,positive(det2). || Expand each coordinate wedge evaluation to the corresponding cross coefficient. || Apply Euclidean norm squared to those three components; area squared equals their square sum. || Swap product expansion; cross terms cancel leaving-2dot. || Subtract swapped products; scalar terms cancel leaving2cross. || Distribute in each slot; swapping negates expression. || Distribute in each slot; C(e1,e1)=1 disproves alternation. || Cubing preserves sign reversal, but A(2e1,e2)=8 rather than2A(e1,e2). || e3,e4 are distinct unit normals, proving nonuniqueness. || Fixed coordinate3-form is defined as determinant evaluation; swapping inputs swaps determinant columns. || ((r+h)²-r²)k/2=rhk+h²k/2; relative error h/(2r), inner arc rk explains r. || Signed area of image unit square is determinant ad-bc. || For a linear grid machine, the image basis parallelepiped has determinant(u,v,w); arbitrary edge-volume scaling is formalized independently in3.4. The omitted linearity should be understood from the grid-machine context, not asserted for arbitrary nonlinear machines. || 13 and-13; ordinary volume13 unchanged. || (e1,e2,e3) and(e2,e1,e3), determinants1,-1. || Six independent coordinate-plane area measurements cannot be represented by the four coordinates of a single normal vector; normal space is two-dimensional for an embedded plane.''')
add(17,'''Expand x=sum xj ej and apply linearity to obtain sum xj ell(ej). || aj=ell(ej) is forced by testing ej, giving the unique dot-product coefficient vector. || For nonzero a, subtract equal-level equations to get a·(p-q)=0; all level sets have the same perpendicular direction space. The section later explicitly separates a=0.''')
add(18,'''T(ae1+be2)=aT(e1)+bT(e2); equal increments give translated identical (possibly degenerate) parallelograms. || Requires T linear: then T(x)=sum xj T(ej). Literally 'nothing else about T' is false: I and N=(x²,y) agree on basis but differ at(2,0). || A ej selects columnj, hence that column is image of thejth input basis vector.''')
add(19,'''Composition sends ej to B(Aej); the product is defined with exactly those columns, whereas addition sums separate outputs. || Rotate90degrees then project e1 gives0; project then rotate gives e2. || An inverse must recover each input uniquely and reach all target outputs; collapsing two inputs to one makes recovery impossible.''')
add(20,'''Ordinary area doubles and ordered basis orientation reverses. || Outputs lie in a line or point because columns are dependent; distinct inputs collide. || Multiply the two signed area factors; algebraic product theorem has already been proved independently by basis expansion.''')
add(21,'''Each is a line; simultaneous solution is their common point. || Row replacement R2+cR1 is undone by R2-cR1, preserving both implications of satisfaction. || Distinct parallel lines:none; intersecting nonparallel:one; coincident:infinitely many.''')
add(22,'''Symmetric coefficients are uniquely fixed by square coefficients and half each mixed coefficient; antisymmetric part contributes zero. || Positive coefficients bound both coordinates on a positive level, making an ellipse; opposite signs permit arbitrarily large cancellation, giving hyperbola. || Using the supplied hint q(x)>0 for nonzero x, the origin is a strict global minimum and every radial slice bends upward.''')
add(23,'''abs(h²)/abs(h)=abs(h)->0, so quadratic error disappears at first-order scale. || Demand norm(error)/norm(h)->0; subtract candidates and restrict h=t u to force(L-M)u=0. || Each of n basis inputs needs m output coordinates: an m-by-n matrix; scalar case n=m=1 is the exception.''')
add(24,'''The map records starting time, direction, clock and repetitions; a circle is not a single y=f(x) graph. || Elimination can discard parameter range, order, direction and repeated visits. || Images must coincide as sets, while monotone clocks, orientation and repetition counts may vary.''')
add(25,'''Angle fixes a ray; radius locates a point along it. || theta+2kpi repeats; at r=0 all angle contributions multiply zero. || Circle fixes distance, spiral relates distance directly to angle; polar variables encode exactly that geometry.''')
add(26,'''rho fixed gives sphere, phi fixed a cone except pi/2 gives plane; theta fixed gives vertical half-plane. || Pipe has an axis and constant cylindrical radius; ball has a center and bounded spherical radius. || Angular lengths scale by circle radii rho and rho sin phi; radial length does not.''')
add(27,'''x²+4y²=9-c: ellipse c<9,point c=9,empty c>9. Complete family identifies downward bowl; finitely many slices do not determine arbitrary surfaces. || A graph has one height for each(x,y), whereas a sphere has two; implicit level sets permit both. || A parametrized curve supplies a time/order; a membership equation supplies neither, and a sheet needs two independent coordinates for local location.''')
add(28,'''Vary angle to circle at fixed height; vary height to vertical line; independent choices require two inputs. || Point(1,sqrt3,2); domain theta in[0,pi], z in[1,4]. || Frozen u=c gives(c,v,cv), frozen v=c gives(u,c,cu), both straight lines. || Sum squares9; theta0 and2pi share meridian, phi0/pi collapse to north/south pole. || Same cylindrical image; away from seam there is one preimage for[0,2pi] and two for[0,4pi].''')
add(29,'''Upper semicircle radius2, from(2,0) to(-2,0), counterclockwise. || Twice, since angle runs0 to4pi. || (-3sqrt3,3). || (2sqrt2,7pi/4). || Circle radius5 centered origin. || x²+(y-3)²=9, circlecenter(0,3),radius3. || (2,2sqrt3,2). || (2,2pi/3,5). || (3sqrt6/4,3sqrt6/4,3/2). || Cylinder radius2 about z-axis. || Sphere radius4 centered origin. || Upper cone z=sqrt3 sqrt(x²+y²). || Circular cylinder radius4. || Upward elliptic paraboloid. || Ellipsoid semiaxes2,3,5. || One-sheet hyperboloid with waistcircle radius2. || Saddle hyperbolic paraboloid. || Sphere radius3 centeredorigin. || Upward circular paraboloid. || Cylindrical side radius3, withheightfree. || (1,2,3)+u(1,0,1)+v(0,2,-1). || (3 sin phi cos theta,3 sin phi sin theta,3 cos phi),theta[0,2pi],phi[0,pi]. || u=c gives(c,v,cv),v=c gives(u,c,cu),straight lines. || Edges theta0/2pi coincide at(2,0,z); theta fixed gives verticalsegments, z fixed gives circles.''')
add(30,'''A vector is precisely its coordinate tuple, so knowing all components at each time determines it. || Each gap is bounded by normgap; conversely finitely many component gaps tendingzero make their square sum tendzero. || Function stores clock, direction and number of visits, invisible in image set.''')
add(31,'''For h>0 secant quotient directions converge to normalized nonzero derivative, the oriented tangent direction. || Reparametrizing scales velocity by clock derivative; its line is geometric while magnitude measures traversal speed. || Antiderivative recovers position up to initial position, one integration constant per coordinate. || alpha(s)=(s,-1,s²+1), beta(t)=(1,t,1+t²); at(1,-1,2), v=(1,0,2),w=(0,1,-2), cross=(-2,2,1). Nonzero and continuous neighboring polynomial velocities give regularity; swapping negates normal.''')
add(32,'''Triangle inequality for polygonal segments, then limit, gives length>=displacement. Equality is monotone traversal of a straight segment, allowing stationary portions and the constant path. || A once-only regular clock change multiplies speed by abs(dt/ds); substitution compensates the parameter interval. Repetitions would change length. || Arc-length derivative hasnorm1, since ds/dt=speed>0 and inverse derivative cancels that speed.''')
add(33,'''Curvature is tangent-direction change per distance; on a regular straight traversal T is constant so kappa0. At stopped reversals the formula is undefined, as the following warning states. || N normalizes T prime, so points toward the side the tangent turns; for a circle this is inward. || Differentiate v=sigma T: sigma prime T changes speed; sigma T prime changes direction, giving kappa sigma² N where curvaturepositive.''')
add(34,'''Cross product is perpendicular to both spanning unitvectors; order fixes right-handed normal and reversing gives -B. || Curvature is a norm and nonnegative; torsion is signed coefficient in B_s=-tau N, giving handedness of osculating-plane rotation.''')
add(35,'''Changing r moves outward/inward; changing theta moves around a circle; these perpendicular contributions add. || Constant magnitude does not imply constant vector: direction e_theta rotates, producing inward acceleration. || For nonzero conserved h, abs(theta prime)=abs(h)/r² and transverse speed abs(h)/r increase as radius decreases. For h=0 there is purely radial motion; the planet illustration presumes an orbit with angular motion.''')
add(36,'''Differentiate exponentials: vx prime=-k vx, vy prime=-k vy, vz prime=-k(vz+g/k)=-k vz-g; initial values match. || Differentiate(1-exp(-kt))/k to exp(-kt); each component reproduces supplied velocity and initial position. || For displayed values z(3)>6,z(4)<-9; repeated interval halving gives landing time 3.4525266573. z prime becomes negative once and stays negative, so this positive landing is unique. Numerical approximation is reproducible using only function evaluation, signs and one-variable monotonicity. || Drag range 53.89561799; no-drag z=1+18t-4.9t² has positive root 3.728209243 and range 74.56418485. || Plot the supplied smooth parametric x(t),z(t) up to landing; no-drag eliminate t=x/20 to get a parabola, drag has exponential/time dependence. || Choose the supplied model road y=.005x² on[-100,100], a permitted concrete path for the project. || Parameterize r(t)=(t,.005t²) on[-100,100], polynomial and regular. || Derivatives(1,.01t),(0,.01), so kappa=.01/(1+.0001t²)^(3/2). || Denominator smallest at t=0; maximum curvature .01. || Choosing supplied A=3 gives local speed bound sqrt(3/kappa); at tightest bend sqrt300≈17.32m/s. This satisfies the speed-limit alternative without unsupplied banking mechanics. || Maximum curvature at vertex is tightest horizontal bend, independent of traversal speed. || Optional spatial extension choose r=(3cos t,3sin t,4t); kappa3/25,tau4/25, so osculating plane turns at constant rate4/25 per length; distinguishes torsion from bend. || v=(1,2t,1/t), a=(0,2,-1/t²), speed sqrt(1+4t²+t^-2). || Point(1,1,0),velocity(1,0,1); line(1,1,0)+s(1,0,1). || Constant speed5, length10pi. || T(1)=(1,2)/sqrt5. || kappa0=2,kappa1=2/(5sqrt5); vertex bends more. || speed sqrt5,T=(-2sin t,2cos t,1)/sqrt5,N=(-cos t,-sin t,0),B=(sin t,-cos t,2)/sqrt5,kappa2/5,tau1/5,aT0,aN2. || radial1, transverse2t(1+t). || radial -27t^4, transverse18t. || A=12(5-1)/2=24. || Differentiate v·v=constant:2v·a=0, including zero-speed constant motion. || .02*15²=4.5m/s². || .5*4²=8. || r prime=(2t,3t²),zero at 0; normalized velocity formula undefined there. || Displacement0,distance4pi. || xy prime-yx prime=R²; integral piR², disk area for positive R. || r third derivative=0 and cross=(0,0,2), so torsion0 everywhere. || speed sqrt(a²+b²); cross=(ab sin t,-ab cos t,a²), triple=a²b, giving kappa=a/(a²+b²),tau=b/(a²+b²). b->-b reverses handedness/torsion, leaving curvature.''')
add(37,'''A scalar field returns one measurement and a vector field a tuple/direction; temperature versus wind. || The domain determines the number of independent inputs and allowable variation, even with the same symbolic rule. || A field picture shows relations between nearby values, such as circulation of (-y,x), absent from one evaluation.''')
add(38,'''Two input coordinates plus one output height require three graph coordinates. || Label c on each curve stores height; finitely many contours leave intervening values free. Equal height increments divided by shorter transverse spacing mean larger average change. || Estimate height difference divided by transverse distance; along one level the value is constant. The level labels and their spacing determine the estimated rate.''')
add(39,'''There are infinitely many straight and curved approaches; the same limiting value must work for all. || Conflicting path limits disprove the all-point condition; agreement along finitely many paths leaves other approaches unchecked. || One delta must control every domain point with 0 < distance < delta, uniformly over directions.''')
add(40,'''Every desired output tolerance can be met by an input tolerance relative to the domain; isolated points satisfy this automatically. || Choose an outer input tolerance for the desired output tolerance, then an inner tolerance placing all F outputs within it. A jump can break that chain. || On the open unit disk, f=x approaches 1 without attaining it; on the whole plane it is unbounded. The supplied EVT requires both closedness and boundedness and a nonempty domain.''')
add(41,'''Parabolic approaches can escape straight-line tests: x²y/(x^4+y²) tends to 0 along every line but is 1/2 along y=x². || The function xy/(x²+y²), extended by 0, restricts to 0 on each axis but is 1/2 along the diagonal. || The narrowing ridge 0<y<x^4 escapes finite pixel resolution; the exact path y=x^4/2 has value 1 away from the origin.''')
add(42,'''A scalar field has one output, such as temperature; a vector field several, such as wind. || Input pair, output triple. || The domain specifies where evaluation and approach are allowed; the same formula can behave differently on restricted sets. || Intersect the graph with z=c and project to the xy-plane: f(x,y)=c. || No: one input cannot have two distinct function values. || Different axis limits disprove existence; equal axis limits do not control diagonal or curved paths. || Set x=r cos(theta), y=r sin(theta). As r tends to 0, the estimate must control all theta, not only fixed theta. || At an accumulation point: f(a) is defined, the limit exists, and the limit equals f(a). The unqualified prompt fails at isolated points: D={a}, f(a)=7 is continuous without a book-defined punctured limit. || Missing boundary points can remove extrema; unbounded sets can allow values to escape. The supplied EVT uses both hypotheses. || Set f=0 off the origin and f(0)=1: the limit is 0 but f is not continuous. || Closed disk of radius 3. || Open disk of radius 2. || All of R3 except the double cone z²=x²+y². || Intersection of x-y>=0 and x+y>0. || Conditions z>=0 and x²+y²!=1. || Exterior x²+y²>1. || Parallel lines x+y=4,3,2,1; graph is an affine plane. || Ellipses x²+9y²=1,9,36. For equal coordinate steps from the center, the y contribution changes nine times as fast. || Spheres centered at (2,-1,0) with radii 1,2,3. || Double cone, two-sheet hyperboloid, one-sheet hyperboloid. || Positive levels are hyperbolas in quadrants I/III; negative levels in II/IV; the zero set is the union of the axes. || Radii 1,2,3,4 give equal radial spacing 1 despite unequal level increments. || Limit 0 by 0<=r²/(1+r²)<=r². || Limit 0: abs(x³+y³)<=r(x²+y²), so the absolute quotient is at most r. || Limit 0: abs(xy)<=r²/2, so the absolute quotient is at most r/2. || No limit: axis limits are 1 and -1. || No limit: y=0 gives 1 and x=0 gives -1. || No limit: y=0 gives 0 and y=x² gives 1/2. || Limit 0: absolute quotient is at most r²/4. || Limit 5/3 by continuity and a nonzero denominator. || Limit 0: absolute value is bounded by r². || Limit (0,0): the second component has absolute value at most abs(y)<=r; the first tends to 0. || Continuous on its domain {x>=y, x²+y²!=4}, including permitted boundary points by relative continuity. || k=0, since the absolute quotient is at most abs(y). || No k works: axis value 0 and diagonal value 1/2 conflict. || log(9-r²) is continuous for abs(r)<3 and all theta; the standard polar convention further requires r>=0. || D is nonempty, closed because it includes its edges, and bounded inside the unit box; apply the supplied EVT. || Bounded but not closed; both closed and bounded; closed but unbounded; bounded but not closed. || Interpolate levels between measured grid values. The hottest sampled point is (2,2), value 30; draw approximate nested contours with tighter spacing near the central neighbors. Finite data do not uniquely determine the true contours. || On y=mx with m!=0, the inequality 0<mx<x^4 fails for sufficiently small nonzero x; the horizontal and vertical axes give value 0. On y=x^4/2 the value is 1 for x!=0. || For f=x²y/(x^4+y²), the paths y=x² and y=2x² give 1/2 and 2/5. || Off the origin the quotient is r, which tends to 0. Set F(0)=0 and use this bound. || Collect a two-input table, plot interpolated level curves, label units and the largest/smallest sampled values, and estimate average change from level differences divided by distance. This task requests data collection; the preceding table activity supplies the procedure, and no measurements are fabricated here.''')
add(43,'''Freezing y gives the ordinary function x -> f(x,b); its derivative is the definition of fx. || Hold y fixed and vary x; this is only the e1 direction. || Coordinate partials do not control simultaneous changes: x²y/(x²+y²), extended by 0, has zero axis partials but diagonal slope 1/2.''')
add(44,'''Split the diagonal step into coordinate steps. The one-variable MVT and continuity of partials replace intermediate slopes by their values at p, leaving fx u1+fy u2. || For nonzero gradient, the dot product is norm(gradient) cos(theta), maximized in the parallel unit direction. Zero gradient gives zero in every direction. || Constancy along the curve gives zero tangent directional derivative. The dot product of a nonzero gradient and nonzero tangent is zero, so they are perpendicular.''')
add(45,'''Axis limits do not control arbitrary increments; diagonals can behave differently. || Require R/norm(h) -> 0 along all approaches, a stronger condition than R -> 0. || Differentiability requires the same affine plane to approximate every small step to first order; fitting the two axes alone is insufficient.''')
add(46,'''The linear functional predicts the first-order change for any small input step; its value 34 answers only one particular step. || The error o(norm(step)) controls asymptotically small steps, not arbitrary finite steps or relative error compared with a vanishing linear part. || At the fixed base point, feed in (a,b) and return 2a-2b. The original function instead takes an input point and returns its value.''')
add(47,'''Each of m output measurements responds to n input coordinates, giving m rows and n columns. || Under differentiability, J maps an input increment to the first-order output increment; the remaining error is o(norm(h)). || det(J) is the signed scale of the derivative linear map. Its absolute value predicts size scaling, its sign orientation, and zero indicates derivative degeneracy. This is a local first-order claim, not an integral substitution theorem.''')
add(48,'''Continuous partials imply differentiability in one direction; failure of continuous partials does not disprove differentiability. The extended function x² sin(1/x) is a counterexample to the converse. || Both axis partials vanish, but on y=x the function G equals x/2; error divided by step length does not tend to zero. || The proposed plane has nonzero first-order error on diagonals. A tangent plane must have error o(distance) along all approaches.''')
add(49,'''Ordinary x slope with y fixed. || A partial uses a coordinate unit direction; a directional derivative uses any specified unit direction. || Otherwise the parameter step mixes direction and speed; normalize for a rate per unit distance. || The dot product projects the gradient onto the unit direction: rate=norm(gradient) cos(angle). || All directional derivatives are zero. || Continuity controls values; differentiability demands error beyond the linear part smaller than the step length. || f(p+h)-f(p)=Lh+o(norm(h)); both terms tend to 0 because L has finite coefficients. || Coordinate MVT telescopes the changes, and continuous partials bound the remainder by a factor tending to 0 times the step length. || A linear functional on displacements returning first-order change. || dx(h,k)=h and dy(h,k)=k. || Rows are (Fi,u,Fi,v); columns are the three component responses to u and v. || Negative signed scale of the derivative means orientation reversal to first order. || The axes give 0 and the diagonal 1/2: discontinuous despite zero axis partials. || abs(g)<=abs(y) proves continuity; axis partials vanish but g(t,t)/sqrt(2t²)=sign(t)/(2sqrt(2)). || fx=2x exp(y)-2xy/(1+x²), fy=x² exp(y)-log(1+x²). At (1,0), these are 2 and 1-log(2). || Domain x²+4y²<=25; in its interior hx=-x/sqrt(25-x²-4y²), hy=-4y/sqrt(25-x²-4y²). Ordinary two-sided partials are not defined on the ellipse boundary. || Tx=-8x/(1+x²+y²)², Ty=-8y/(1+x²+y²)², Tz=-.7; at the stated point the gradient is (-2,0,-.7). || Gradient (2x,2y,2z)/(x²+y²+z²), valid except at the origin. || Cu=3+.01v=4, Cv=4+.01u=6; output units per unit u and per unit v, respectively. Concrete unit names are not specified. || Mx=.2+.03y, My=-.4+.03x; at (10,5) these are .35 and -.1, so M rises with x and falls with y. || Wx=(0,1), Wy=(-1,0). || Rows (1,1,0), (0,z,y), (2x,0,-1). || Gradient (0,11); normalized direction (2,-1)/sqrt(5) gives -11/sqrt(5). || Gradient (2,0), dotted with (1,1)/sqrt(2), gives sqrt(2). || Gradient (-1,-2); fastest direction (-1,-2)/sqrt(5), rate sqrt(5). || Direction (1,2)/sqrt(5). || Gradient (-2,0,-.7), directional rate -.56=-14/25. || Gradient (-2,5,-4); unit direction (1,2,2)/3 gives rate 0. || Directional rate -26/5. || Zero in all directions. || L=2+2(x-1); estimate 2.04. || z=sqrt(23)-(2/sqrt(23))(x-2)-(3/sqrt(23))(y-3). || df=2xy dx+x² dy+cos(z) dz; at (1,2,0), df=4dx+dy+dz. || dV=15dx+10dy+6dz=.15-.2+.24=.19. || dS=.5da-2db; predicted change -.065. || Axis partials vanish; on (t,t), f=t/2 and error/step length=sign(t)/(2sqrt(2)), which does not vanish. || At the origin L=0 because abs(f)/sqrt(x²+y²)<=abs(x). For x!=0, fx=2x sin(1/x)-cos(1/x), which oscillates instead of approaching fx(0)=0. || Neither axis partial exists because abs(t)/t has opposite one-sided limits. Differentiability would force partials, so fails. || J=[[2x,-1],[y,x+2y]]. At (1,2), F=(-1,6), J=[[2,-1],[2,5]], and F≈(-1,6)+J(h,k). || J=[[1,1,1],[2x,-z,-y]], a 2-by-3 matrix. || J columns are (1,0,u/2),(0,1,-2v/9); at (2,3), (1,0,1),(0,1,-2/3). || Base point (2,3,2); plane equals the base plus a(1,0,1)+b(0,1,-2/3). || Determinant 5, signed area scale factor 5. || Determinant 2x+1, vanishing at x=-1/2. || J=[[cos(theta),-r sin(theta)],[sin(theta),r cos(theta)]], determinant r. || Expand the wedge of the two one-forms: repeated dr and dtheta factors vanish; the remaining coefficient is r(cos²(theta)+sin²(theta))=r. || (dx+dy) wedge (dx-dy)=-2 dx wedge dy, reversing orientation. || J=[[1,1],[1,-1],[v,u]], a 3-by-2 matrix; a nonsquare matrix has no determinant. || T in Celsius, p in percentage points, s in fish/m³, O in mg/L. || Choose the locally supplied base point (22,60,30). || OT=-.18-.002(s-30), Op=.035, Os=-.025-.002(T-22); at the base these are (-.18,.035,-.025). || dO=-.18dT+.035dp-.025ds. || Choose increments (2,5,-3); the linear prediction is -.11 mg/L. || The exact change is -.098; the quadratic cross contribution is +.012. || For these increments the effect magnitudes .36,.175,.075 rank T,p,s. Raw partials measured in different units are not comparable. || Report the local model response and omitted volume/plants/time/feed effects; make no unsupported global causal claim. || For a unit direction (a,b), f(ta,tb)=t a³, so the directional derivative is a³. Partials (1,0) would force L=x, but at a=b=1/sqrt(2), a³!=a. The directional data are not linear, hence f is not differentiable. || J=diag(2x,1), determinant 2x. Reflection pairs (+x,y),(-x,y) have identical images, folding onto output x>=0. || J=[[1,k],[0,1]], determinant 1. Image vertices (0,0),(1,0),(1+k,1),(k,1) give base and height 1. || J(1,2)=[[3,1],[-2,1]]; rows are 3dx+dy and -2dx+dy, columns (3,-2),(1,1). || The candidate plane z=0 fails on y=x with first-order error t/2 despite perfect axis fit. || Use G=x²y/(x²+y²), extended by 0: abs(G)<=abs(y), axis partials vanish, but the diagonal error quotient does not. || df=2xy dx+(x²-3y²)dy; at (2,1), df=4dx+dy, evaluating to .2 on the specified displacement. Differentiability gives the first-order prediction. || (3dx-dy) wedge (2dx+4dy)=14 dx wedge dy; determinant 14 follows by bilinear alternating expansion.''')
add(50,'''The differential is linear: simultaneous coordinate contributions add as fx dx+fy dy; substitute the coordinate velocities. || The dot product is the sum of matching component products. || Yes: move perpendicular to the gradient, or stand still. Conversely a constant field has zero gradient and cannot give a nonzero rate due to motion.''')
add(51,'''The displacement passes first through JG then JF, so the product is JF JG; matrix addition would not compose the maps. || Multiply derivatives along each dependency route from u to w, then sum over the routes. || A fixed linear map has a bounded norm factor. The inner displacement is O(norm(h)), so the inner and outer remainders remain o(norm(h)).''')
add(52,'''Differentials are linear maps; composing linear coordinate expressions is ordinary substitution and distribution. || On an input vector, first apply DG, then measure using omega at the image point. The resulting functional lives on the input space. || The identity states composition of the actual linear functionals; changing coordinate labels does not change it. No integration theorem is needed.''')
add(53,'''Ftheta is a rate per radian. Tangential displacement has length r dtheta, giving rate per distance Ftheta/r; the coordinate basis also changes. || A determinant measures areas under a linear map; the Jacobian is the derivative matrix, so gives the local parallelogram prediction. || Varying theta at the origin/axis leaves the physical point unchanged: a coordinate parameter direction collapses.''')
add(54,'''The chain rule for G composed with F=I gives DG DF=I. Thus DG is the matrix inverse, not entrywise reciprocals. || A smooth local inverse does not exclude distant inputs sharing an output; the exponential polar map repeats at v+2pi. || A singular linear part kills a nonzero vector, so first-order data cannot recover that input direction. Nonlinear injectivity can still hold.''')
add(55,'''IFT supplies a differentiable local graph g; the chain rule on F(x,g(x))=0 gives Fx+Fy gprime=0. || Fy!=0 makes the augmented map (x,F) nonsingular and gives a unique nearby graph. The gradient normal has nonzero y component, so its tangent is not vertical. || The oval endpoint has two nearby branches and no graph over an open x interval; crossing lines y=±x have two outputs for nonzero x. Vanishing Fy alone would not prove failure.''')
add(56,'''A partial captures one coordinate change; the total derivative is a linear function of all increments with remainder o(norm(h)). || Evaluate the gradient at the current image point r(t). || Composition order and matrix dimensions matter; the products need not commute and may not both be defined. || An input increment (du,dv) produces first-order old-coordinate change xu du+xv dv. || Substitute the coefficient functions P(x(u,v),y(u,v)), Q(x(u,v),y(u,v)), and replace dx,dy by their input differentials. || Angles are in radians; tangential length per radian is r. || It is the signed scale of the derivative linear map; ordinary scale is abs(det). || The local C1 theorem controls a nearby neighborhood; periodicity can repeat outputs beyond it. || A graph y=g(x), assuming the base point lies on the zero set and the C1 hypotheses hold. || On the implicit graph, zx=-Fx/Fz and zy=-Fy/Fz. || The differential annihilates allowed tangent displacements. || F(u,v)=(exp(u)cos(v),exp(u)sin(v)) has determinant exp(2u)>0 but is periodic in v. || At t=0, point (1,pi/2), velocity (0,-1), gradient (pi,1), giving rate -1. || At t=0, point (1,1,2), velocity (1,-1,0), gradient (-1,1,3), giving rate -2. || Rate 4-2-6=-4. || zu=2u fx+fy, zv=fx-2v fy, with partials evaluated at the intermediate point. || wu=v fx+fy+2u fz, wv=u fx+fy-2v fz. || G(1,2)=(3,2); JF=[[6,1],[2,3]], JG=[[1,1],[2,1]], product [[8,7],[8,5]]. || G(1,1)=(2,0,1); JF=(1,0,-1), JG rows (2,1),(1,-2),(1,1), product (1,0). || du=2x dx+dy, dv=dx-2y dy. || The composition is exp(u²-v²), with differential exp(u²-v²)(2u du-2v dv). || Pullback 2u²v dv-2uv² du. || (du+dv) wedge (2u du-dv)=-(1+2u) du wedge dv; collapse occurs at u=-1/2. || F=r² cos²(theta)-3r sin(theta); Fr=2r cos²(theta)-3sin(theta), Ftheta=-2r² cos(theta)sin(theta)-3r cos(theta). || F=r² sin(theta)cos(theta); Ftheta/r=r cos(2theta), equal to 0 at the stated point. || P=r²-z; derivatives are 2r,0,-1. || The expression is rho²; angular variables are absent, so angular derivatives vanish. || Determinant 1+4uv; the C1 polynomial map has a local inverse where uv!=-1/4. || Determinant exp(2u), nonzero everywhere; periodicity prevents global injectivity. || Inverse matrix [[1,-1],[-1,2]], conditional on a smooth local inverse. || F(3,3)=0 and Fy=Fx=9; a local graph exists and gprime=-1. || The zero set consists of two intersecting lines y=±x, so is not one graph locally. || Exactly where z!=0 on the sphere, zx=-x/z and zy=-y/z. At the equator, nearby vertical lines inside the projection have points with both signs of z, so the zero set is not one graph there. || z=(4-x-y)/(1+xy) near (1,1), with gx=gy=-1. || Normal (2,8,6); plane 2(x-1)+8(y-2)+6(z-1)=0. || The augmented 4-by-4 Jacobian has an upper identity block and lower (u,v) block [[2,1],[1,-2]], giving determinant -5. Apply the supplied n-dimensional IFT and then fix the last two outputs to 0. || Differentiate both constraints in x: 2ux+vx=-1, ux-2vx=0. Thus ux=-2/5 and vx=-1/5. || Q=x²y/(x²+y²), extended by 0, has zero axis partials but the path (t,t) gives derivative 1/2 instead of 0. The differentiability hypothesis of the chain rule fails.''')
